Contact: Target runtime 42 minutes (12-minute cut: see diamond-anaconda-12min-cut.md). No narration. No voiceover.
Notes: HERO ELEMENT = 066aa34a-37fa-411b-8dcf-d356fb5bf083 (Ryan-Cole-Armed). DO NOT USE e788b2c7-0476-443f-bfdb-dd8832f85644 (retired, unarmed) — it renders Ryan with no rifle.

====

[[ HERO ELEMENT: 066aa34a-37fa-411b-8dcf-d356fb5bf083 — Ryan-Cole-Armed. NEVER e788b2c7 (retired, unarmed). Ryan's rifle is slung across his back in every tunnel scene. DIAMOND PROP: e0762233-1eaa-43ab-a8d6-5c99fa8e0a0b — 8cm, irregular. CREATURE: 88a74648-e818-438f-ab04-5ba44a56cabf — FIFTY FEET, 60cm thick, fixed. NEVER 223a47e0 (retired, 30ft). ]]

INT. UNDERGROUND TUNNEL - NIGHT

*[DEPTH 11 — the deepest tunnel. Ankle-deep still water. One torch, handheld, failing. Roof low enough to touch. Handprints.]*

Black.

Breathing. Wet, ragged, close.

Water dripping somewhere enormous.

Boots through shallow water. A flashlight beam swings up and finds the wall.

HANDPRINTS. Dozens of them. Blood, dried black, smeared along the rock.

All of them travelling one direction. Deeper into the mountain.

Nobody was crawling out.

RYAN COLE, 50s, blood down one side of his face, moves along the wall in the same direction as the prints.

His rifle is slung across his back where he cannot reach it quickly. Torch in one hand, pack on the other shoulder. He has carried that weapon through everything and it has not once been any use to him.

He stops. Listens.

Far behind him, down the tunnel — a smudge of grey. Daylight.

He looks at it a long moment.

Then he turns his back on it.

He unslings his pack. Takes out a DIAMOND about the size of a hen's egg — six centimetres of rough crystal, heavy in the hand, no bigger than that and no less astonishing for it.

It throws light across the whole tunnel — and the walls answer. Thousands of points, seamed through the rock in clusters, a cathedral of them.

He crouches. Sets the diamond down on the wet stone.

Carefully. Almost gently.

And backs away from it.

HOLD on the abandoned diamond in the water.

For half a second — barely enough to register — something inside it MOVES. A thin dark line, turning over.

A HISS. Deep. Wet. Behind him.

Something enormous shifts its weight, and the water in the tunnel moves.

Ryan does not run. He watches the diamond.

Two EYES rise out of the black.

They go to the stone on the ground.

Not to him.

RYAN
(whisper)
We were never supposed to find them.

He turns and RUNS — deeper. Away from the daylight.

CUT TO BLACK.

TITLE: DIAMOND ANACONDA

TITLE: 72 HOURS EARLIER.

EXT. FISHING SETTLEMENT - DUSK

Twenty houses on stilts over brown water. Nets. Diesel. A fire burning low in an oil drum.

ARMANDO, 70s, sits beside it. Ryan sits across from him — clean, dry, out of place.

Between them on a crate: an old photograph, curling. A jungle-covered island shot from a boat.

ARMANDO
You want me to tell you it's a story.

RYAN
I want you to tell me what it is.

ARMANDO
I'll say it's a story, if that's what gets you on the boat and out of my chair.

Ryan doesn't smile. Armando likes that.

ARMANDO (CONT'D)
My grandfather cut cane on that island. Nineteen men went out. Six came back.

RYAN
Poor.

ARMANDO
Rich. They came back rich, and not one of them ever went near water again. My grandfather washed in a bucket until the day he died. Indoors.

Ryan turns the photograph toward the light.

RYAN
And the snake.

ARMANDO
She was there before the cane. Before the Spanish. They call her the mother.

RYAN
That's a lot of respect for an animal.

ARMANDO
It's not respect. It's manners.

He leans in.

ARMANDO (CONT'D)
She goes down into the island. Months. Years. Nobody sees her. And then she comes up, and she brings them out of her. Out of her belly, onto the stones.

RYAN
Snakes don't do that.

ARMANDO
Neither do rocks.

Beat. Ryan sits back.

RYAN
Alright.

ARMANDO
You don't believe me.

RYAN
I believe you believe you.

Armando reaches into his shirt pocket. Puts something small on the crate between them.

A STONE. Tiny — a centimetre, no more, the size of a pea on the wood. Rough, unfinished, and it catches the firelight like a struck match.

Ryan stops moving.

He picks it up. Turns it. Holds it against the fire.

RYAN (CONT'D)
Where did you get this.

ARMANDO
My grandfather's bucket.

Ryan can't take his eyes off it.

ARMANDO (CONT'D)
That is the smallest one.

Ryan looks up.

RYAN
How do you know?

ARMANDO
Because I've seen the others.

He takes the stone back. Puts it in his pocket. Pats the pocket.

ARMANDO (CONT'D)
Take your friends. Take your guns. Take whatever you like off that island.

He pokes the fire.

ARMANDO (CONT'D)
Just don't take it out of the hole.

EXT. BOATYARD - DAY

A rented shell on a working dock. Gear laid out on the concrete in seven piles.

Seven people, packing.

MARCUS, 58, sits on an upturned crate cleaning a rifle he has owned for thirty years. He does not look at his hands.

KOFI MENSAH, 40s, feeds a belt into an ammunition box for a light machine gun. Unhurried. He has not said a word since he arrived.

MATEO ALVAREZ, 30s, tapes charges into a padded bag, talking the whole time.

MATEO
Two years. Two years of braces. You know what that costs, in America?

LIAM O'ROURKE, 26, films him on a small camera. Films everything.

LIAM
Tell the camera.

MATEO
It costs a house, Liam. My daughter has a house in her mouth.

DANIEL MERCER, 40s, counts a stack of nothing on a table, out loud.

DANIEL
Seven ways. That's fourteen percent each.

MARCUS
(not looking up)
That's not how sevenths work.

DANIEL
It is if I'm doing the math.

MAYA BENNETT, 30s, kneels over a medical roll, packing blood-clotting agent. A lot of it. A jeweller's loupe sits on the concrete beside her knee, which is not standard medical kit.

Ryan crouches beside her.

RYAN
That's four times what you need.

MAYA
You said no contact.

RYAN
There won't be.

MAYA
Then you won't mind me being wrong.

She keeps packing. Ryan lets it go.

Liam swings the camera onto Marcus.

LIAM
Marcus. Say something for the camera.

MARCUS
No.

LIAM
Anything.

MARCUS
No.

Liam swings to Maya, who has been silent for a full minute.

LIAM
Maya. Say some—

MAYA
Ryan.

Something in her voice. The room turns.

MAYA (CONT'D)
Where did this come from.

She's holding Armando's stone up in a pair of tweezers. Ryan hasn't seen her take it.

RYAN
An old man's table.

MAYA
That's a location. I asked where it came from.

RYAN
Is there a difference?

MAYA
There's a billion years of difference.

She sets the loupe down. Now she looks at him.

MAYA (CONT'D)
A diamond forms one way. Deep, hot, under a hundred and fifty kilometres of rock, and it grows as one crystal. One. Direction.

She turns the stone.

MAYA (CONT'D)
This has layers. It grew in rings. Like a tree.

DANIEL
So it's a bad diamond.

MAYA
It's not a bad diamond, Daniel, it's a spectacular diamond, it's just not a diamond.

Beat.

MAYA (CONT'D)
This did not come out of the ground.

Daniel laughs. Mateo laughs. Liam films Maya's face.

DANIEL
Then where'd it come from, the sky?

Maya doesn't answer. She's still looking at it.

Ryan takes the stone back off her, gently, and puts it in his pocket.

RYAN
We go in, we find them, we get out.

DANIEL
(zipping his bag)
What could possibly go wrong.

EXT. BOATYARD - NIGHT

Water slapping pilings. The boat loaded. Everyone else asleep or pretending to be.

Maya sits on a bollard. Ryan comes down the dock with two coffees. Hands her one.

They sit a while.

MAYA
This is my last one.

RYAN
You said that in Kandahar.

MAYA
I said it drunk in Kandahar. I'm saying it sober on a dock.

She wraps both hands around the cup.

MAYA (CONT'D)
Say it back to me.

RYAN
Maya.

MAYA
Say it back or I get off the boat. I'm not being cute, Ryan, I will physically get off the boat.

He looks at her. She means it.

RYAN
This is your last one.

MAYA
And?

RYAN
...And I bring you home.

MAYA
There it is.

She drinks her coffee.

MAYA (CONT'D)
Now you've said it out loud. Now it's a thing you have to do.

EXT. RIVER MOUTH - DAY

AERIAL: the island. Enormous. Green to the waterline, black channels cutting into it like veins. No smoke. No roofs. No roads.

The boat enters the river mouth and the jungle closes over it.

The light goes flat.

Nobody says anything for a while.

Then the ENGINE dies. Just stops.

Mateo is at the motor immediately.

MATEO
It's not the fuel.

MARCUS
Didn't say it was.

MATEO
It's not anything. It's just off.

The boat drifts on the current. Liam films the treeline.

And then Maya notices it.

MAYA
Stop talking.

They stop.

Nothing. No birds. No frogs. No insects.

The most silent place any of them have ever been.

MARCUS
(quietly)
Where did everything go?

Ryan looks up.

Above them, hanging in the mangroves, catching the light like paper —

SHED SNAKESKINS.

Not one. Dozens. Most of them eight or ten feet, hanging like paper.

And two that are not. Those two are draped over branches thirty feet up, and they run the length of three of the others.

Mateo stands up in the boat very slowly.

KOFI
(quietly, counting)
Nine. Eleven. Fourteen.

DANIEL
Okay.

MAYA
Ryan.

RYAN
I see them.

DANIEL
Okay, so those are — those are shed skins, that's — snakes shed, that's a normal —

MAYA
Daniel.

DANIEL
— that's a normal thing that snakes do —

MARCUS
Son. Look how high they are.

Daniel looks up. Stops talking.

Ryan is watching the treeline. Not the skins. The dark between them.

RYAN
We're not alone.

EXT. ABANDONED SETTLEMENT - DAY

A dozen rotted structures on a mud shelf above the river. A collapsed dock. Crates furred with moss. A place abandoned in a hurry a long time ago.

The team moves through it in pairs, clearing.

Liam films a doorway. Mateo pulls a tarp off a crate — canned food, rusted through.

Maya crouches in what was a radio hut. She drags a heavy green case into the light.

A military FIELD RADIO. Nineteen-sixties.

MAYA
Ryan.

He comes over. She has the case open.

MAYA (CONT'D)
There's charge in it.

DANIEL
(passing, not stopping)
There's no charge in it. It's been sitting in a swamp since Nixon.

MAYA
The needle's moving.

That stops him.

The team gathers. Maya's hand on the dial.

She turns it on.

STATIC. Loud, then quiet as she thins it out.

She works across the band. Static. Static.

Then — very faint, very close-mic'd, a WOMAN'S VOICE, exhausted:

RADIO VOICE (V.O.)
— don't go underground —

Silence in the settlement.

The static swallows it.

Maya works the dial. Nothing. She works it again.

LIAM
Play it again.

MAYA
It's not a recording, Liam.

Nobody has an answer to that.

Ryan stands up.

RYAN
Perimeter. Two on, four off. We move at first light.

EXT. SETTLEMENT - FIRE - NIGHT

A small fire in a drum. Marcus feeds it. Ryan sits across from him, not sleeping.

Behind them, Mateo is on watch at the waterline with Liam, talking low. We can hear it faintly — the rhythm of a man talking about his kid.

Marcus doesn't look up.

MARCUS
You're doing the thing where you count everybody twice.

Ryan says nothing.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
Kunar.

RYAN
Don't.

MARCUS
I dug two of those holes with you. I get to say the word.

The fire pops.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
You pushed one more klick because you thought the road was clear.

RYAN
It wasn't clear.

MARCUS
No. It wasn't.

He sets another piece of wood on.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
So. When you get the feeling on this rock. When you want to go one more klick.

He finally looks at Ryan.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
Come find me first.

RYAN
That an order?

MARCUS
It's a favour. You'd argue with an order.

Ryan almost smiles.

Then — from the waterline —

LIAM (O.S.)
Mateo?

The two of them are up before the second word.

EXT. RIVERBANK - CONTINUOUS

Liam stands alone in his own torchlight, turning in a circle.

LIAM
He was right here. He was right here, he was talking, I turned around —

Marcus's light finds it first.

MATEO'S RIFLE. Lying at the waterline, half in the shallows.

Maya reaches it first, drops beside it.

MAYA
Mateo!

Nothing. The jungle does not answer. It does not do anything at all.

DANIEL
(picking up the rifle)
Safety's on.

MARCUS
He never got to it.

Daniel looks at the weapon in his hands like it's lying to him.

Maya swings her light down the bank.

MAYA
There's no blood.

DANIEL
There's no drag marks either. There's no — where are the marks?

Ryan's light finds them.

Not marks. A TROUGH. Two feet wide, smooth-bottomed, and pressed six inches deep — running unbroken from the treeline into the water.

Something very heavy went through here, slowly, and did not hurry.

Everybody's light arrives on it at once.

MAYA
(still shouting, breaking)
MATEO!

And out in the black water, something goes past under the surface — a long ridge of displaced water, moving upriver.

It draws level with the bow of their boat.

It keeps going.

It is still going when the far end of it finally clears the stern.

Marcus watches it the whole way, with no expression at all.

MARCUS
That boat is thirty feet.

RYAN
Out of the water.

Nobody moves.

RYAN (CONT'D)
OUT OF THE WATER. NOW.

EXT. DEEP JUNGLE - DAY

Six of them now. Moving in file, weapons up, nobody talking.

The ground gets softer. Kofi stops at a low place where the mud has held its shape. He crouches. Puts two fingers in it.

KOFI
Ryan.

An IMPRESSION in the mud. Two feet across. Smooth-bottomed, and pressed deep enough to hold water.

It comes out of the treeline on one side and goes into the treeline on the other, and there is no beginning to it and no end.

DANIEL
That's a fallen tree. Something dragged a fallen tree through here.

Kofi doesn't argue. He just walks twenty feet along it and points.

The impression bends. Then bends again.

KOFI
Trees don't corner.

He straightens up, and for the first time on this island he unslings the machine gun and carries it in his hands.

EXT. FLOODED CROSSING - DAY

The jungle floor is gone. Brown water, flat and slow, running between the trunks as far as any of them can see.

There is no way around it.

RYAN
Weapons up. Spacing. Nobody bunches.

They go in.

WAIST DEEP. Six of them in file, rifles held high and dry above the surface, moving slow because slow is the only speed the water allows.

The discipline is beautiful. Even now. Even here. Every man scanning his own arc.

LIAM
(low)
How deep does it get?

MARCUS
Deeper.

They wade. Water pushing round their ribs. Insects on the surface. Nothing else.

DANIEL
Keep it dry, Liam. Wet rifle's a stick.

MAYA
Ryan.

RYAN
I know.

MAYA
We are the only thing moving out here.

And Kofi stops.

Dead still, chest-deep, one hand flat on the surface of the water.

The others stop behind him.

Silence.

MARCUS
(barely)
Kofi.

Kofi doesn't answer. He is listening with his hand.

KOFI
...Nothing.

RYAN
Move.

They move.

And the camera LIFTS.

Up off the water. Up past the men, past their raised weapons, past the tops of their heads. Up through a gap in the canopy, higher, until the six of them are six small shapes strung out across a sheet of brown water.

No music. No score. Just the sound of six men wading, getting smaller.

AERIAL. STRAIGHT DOWN.

And from the left of frame, under the surface, SHE comes.

A long dark shape sliding through the flooded forest on a course that will take her directly across their line.

She is unhurried. She is not hunting. She is simply going somewhere.

She reaches them.

And she passes.

Fifty feet of her, four feet under the surface, gliding between Kofi and Daniel — close enough that Daniel's own wake breaks over her back — and neither man turns his head.

She keeps going. Out through the trees. Gone into the green.

The six shapes wade on across the water, in perfect order, scanning their arcs, and not one of them knows.

HOLD.

Then the camera comes DOWN — all the way back to water level, to the sound of men breathing and water moving.

And it is exactly as it was.

Nothing happened.

They reach the far bank. They climb out. They check their weapons.

DANIEL
(shaking water off his rifle, almost cheerful)
Well, that was the worst part.

Nobody answers him.

EXT. 1974 CAMP - DAY

A clearing that was once a camp. Tent frames standing, canvas long gone. Wooden crates stencilled with a company name and a year: 1974.

Expedition gear laid out where men set it down and never came back for it.

And bones. Human. Not buried. Not arranged. Just where they fell, in the leaf litter, gone to moss.

Maya crouches over one. She doesn't touch it.

MAYA
Eleven, maybe twelve.

MARCUS
Same as us. Professionals.

MAYA
How do you know?

MARCUS
(nodding at the crates)
Look how they stacked their kit.

Liam lifts a NOTEBOOK out of an open crate. Swollen, black with mould. He gets it open.

LIAM
There's writing.

He turns it toward the light and reads aloud.

LIAM (CONT'D)
"March eleven. We have confirmed the deposit."

He turns pages, skimming.

LIAM (CONT'D)
Supply lists. Weather. Supply lists.

He stops.

LIAM (CONT'D)
"March nineteen. The diamonds are real."

Nobody moves.

He turns the page.

LIAM (CONT'D)
"The diamonds are not what killed us."

Daniel steps closer.

DANIEL
Killed. Past tense. He wrote that about himself.

Liam turns the page.

Torn. A ragged stub in the spine — the rest of the notebook removed.

LIAM
It's gone. The rest is torn out.

MAYA
By who?

MARCUS
By whoever was still alive to tear it.

Daniel takes the notebook out of Liam's hands. Turns it over. Shakes it, like more will fall out.

DANIEL
This is a hoax. This is — somebody put this here.

MAYA
Somebody put the bones here too?

DANIEL
(too loud)
I'm just saying somebody could have—

And the canopy comes apart above them.

EXT. 1974 CAMP - CONTINUOUS

A SNAKE drops out of the trees — nine feet, thick as a forearm — and lands across Daniel's shoulders and takes him down.

Then another. Then two more.

CHAOS.

Marcus shoots the one on Daniel off him at four feet, clean, without hesitating.

MARCUS
BACK TO BACK. BACK TO BACK!

Maya drags Daniel clear by his webbing. Maya fires into the leaf litter where the ground is moving.

Liam is scrambling backward, and his hands find the drone case, and he does the only thing he knows how to do — he throws it up.

The DRONE screams up out of the clearing.

LIAM
Up up up up —

He's got the controller. He's got the screen. He's shooting.

RYAN
LIAM, MOVE!

LIAM
I've got it — I've got —

He looks at his screen.

And his face changes.

ON THE SCREEN: eighty feet up, looking down through a gap in the canopy at a bend of open bank.

And it is lying there. In daylight. All of it, head to tail, in one frame, for the first time.

Not coiled. Stretched out along the mud, unhurried, the last four feet of it still trailing in the water.

LIAM (CONT'D)
(barely audible)
Ryan. Look at the screen.

RYAN
LIAM!

Liam drags one finger down the glass, measuring it against the hull tied up at the bank behind her.

LIAM
That's our boat. That's our boat right there.

He can't make his voice work properly.

LIAM (CONT'D)
She's half as long again as the boat.

The leaf litter behind Liam lifts.

He is taken so fast that the controller is still in the air when he's gone.

The DRONE, no hands on it now, drifts sideways over the trees.

It goes down in shallow water at the river's edge.

Lens up. Rotor bent. Red light blinking.

Still recording.

EXT. SINKHOLE - DUSK

An opening in the jungle floor forty feet across. Old stone steps cut into one side, going down into the dark, worn concave by a very long time.

Five of them stand at the lip. Daniel has a field dressing on his shoulder and hasn't spoken in an hour.

MAYA
The radio said don't go underground.

DANIEL
The radio is a box of wire from nineteen sixty.

MAYA
And something used it.

Nobody has an answer.

Ryan is looking down the steps.

RYAN
The river's the only way off this island, and the river's got that in it. We're not walking into black water tonight.

MAYA
So we go down.

RYAN
Down is where they are.

MAYA
Listen to yourself.

RYAN
Say it plainly.

MAYA
Mateo is gone. Liam is gone. You are standing at a hole in the ground doing arithmetic about diamonds.

Silence.

Marcus, quietly, from behind them:

MARCUS
One more klick.

Ryan turns. Marcus is looking straight at him.

He is not angry. That's what makes it land.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
I asked you to come find me first.

Long beat.

RYAN
One more klick.

And he goes down the steps.

Maya follows. Then Daniel. Maya stands there a second longer, then goes.

Marcus goes last, and takes one look back at the treeline before he does.

INT. CAVERN - CONTINUOUS

*[DEPTH 2 — dry. Torchlight only, and it does not reach the far wall. Roof lost in the dark. Dust hanging in the beams.]*

Five lights in a space too big for them.

The beams sweep — and the walls answer.

Ten thousand points of light. Diamonds seamed through black rock in clusters, ropes, whole rivers of them running up into the dark.

The most beautiful thing any of them will ever see.

For a long moment nobody speaks. There is only breathing.

DANIEL
(barely)
Oh my God.

He starts to laugh. Puts a hand over his mouth.

DANIEL (CONT'D)
Oh my God. Oh my God, Ryan.

MAYA
Don't touch the walls.

DANIEL
Maya. We came four thousand miles to touch the walls.

He touches the walls.

He works one loose. It comes away in his palm — the size of a hen's egg, heavier than it looks — and the light off it moves across all five of their faces.

Maya is the only one not looking at it. She's looking at the dark at the far end.

MAYA
Ryan. How far back does this go?

Ryan puts his light down the chamber.

It doesn't reach the end.

INT. CARVING CHAMBER - LATER

*[DEPTH 3 — dry, and narrow enough that two of them cannot pass. Roof at head height. Torches close on stone.]*

Narrower. Older. The team's lights find the wall — and stop.

CARVINGS. Thousands. Serpent marks cut by human hands, layer over layer over layer, generations deep. Some fresh-looking. Some worn to nothing.

And at the centre, twenty feet of it, one enormous carved COIL.

Maya moves along the wall with her light.

MAYA
These aren't decoration.

RYAN
No.

MAYA
These are tallies. Somebody was keeping score.

RYAN
For a long time.

Daniel has stopped laughing.

DANIEL
Score of what?

Nobody answers him.

Ryan puts his hand flat against the carved coil. Feels the depth of the cut.

RYAN
This isn't where it lives.

MAYA
Then what is it?

RYAN
It's where it comes back to.

INT. SIDE PASSAGE - LATER

*[DEPTH 4 — damp. A seam, not a room. Kofi is sideways in it.]*

Kofi's light, moving alone down a narrow seam in the rock.

KOFI
(calling back)
Ryan.

INT. SIDE PASSAGE - CONTINUOUS

They gather. Five lights on one shape on the floor.

DANIEL. On his side, curled, one arm underneath him.

Maya is on him fast, two fingers at the throat, then the wrist, then she stops.

MARCUS
How long?

MAYA
Two hours. Maybe less.

She rolls him gently onto his back and runs her hands over him. Nothing. She checks his throat, his ribs, his legs.

MAYA (CONT'D)
There's no wound.

RYAN
Look again.

MAYA
Ryan, there's nothing. No puncture, no constriction bruising, no blood, nothing broke, nothing bit him.

She sits back on her heels.

MAYA (CONT'D)
He ran until his heart stopped.

Nobody speaks.

Kofi crouches and lifts the arm that was underneath him.

There's a sock in Daniel's fist. Something heavy in it.

He tips it out into his palm.

A DIAMOND. Egg-sized, the same as the others. Taken off the wall and hidden from the rest of them.

Marcus looks at it for a long moment.

MARCUS
He was the only one carrying one loose.

The sentence sits there.

And then — without anyone saying anything — Kofi looks down at his own pack.

INT. CAVERN - CAMP - NIGHT

*[DEPTH 5 — a chemical lamp, green-white and unmoving, the only steady light in the film. Water standing in flat pools.]*

Four of them around a chemical lamp in an enormous dark room.

Maya has Daniel's diamond in front of her and the loupe screwed into her eye. She has been at it a long time.

MARCUS
Say it or don't, Maya. Don't do both.

MAYA
It's warm.

RYAN
It was against his body for six hours.

MAYA
On one side.

She turns it. Holds the flat of her hand against it.

MAYA (CONT'D)
It's warm on the inside.

Ryan sits up.

MAYA (CONT'D)
There's no lattice. It's layered — concentric, laid down in rings, from the outside in, the way a shell mineralises.

KOFI
A shell.

MAYA
And there's a mass at the centre.

She sets it down very carefully, like it might roll.

MAYA (CONT'D)
Ryan. She doesn't bring them up out of her belly.

Beat.

MAYA (CONT'D)
She lays them.

Nobody moves.

KOFI
Anacondas don't lay eggs.

Maya looks up at him.

KOFI (CONT'D)
My uncle kept them. They carry them. They give birth to them alive, in the water. Every one of them.

MAYA
I know.

She looks back down at the thing on the rock in front of her.

MAYA (CONT'D)
So whatever's down here with us — it isn't an anaconda.

Ryan looks — slowly — at the four packs sitting in a row against the rock, bulging, forty pounds each.

RYAN
Open your packs.

MARCUS
Ryan—

RYAN
Open them. Now.

They open them.

And the lamp catches them — dozens, in every bag, warm from being carried against their spines all day.

They have been sleeping in her nest with her children in their arms.

KOFI
(very quietly)
Ah.

And somewhere out in the dark of the chamber, a long way off, something very large begins to move.

INT. CAVERN - PASSAGES - CONTINUOUS

*[DEPTH 6 — ankle-deep and running. Nothing but swinging torchlight and muzzle flash.]*

RUNNING. Lights swinging. Four people through a rock throat.

And the floor comes alive.

HATCHLINGS — four, six, nine feet, fast, dozens of them — coming out of the seams in the walls.

Kofi opens up with the machine gun and the noise in the enclosed rock is apocalyptic.

MARCUS
LEFT! LEFT!

They fight through a junction. Maya drags Ryan off a wall. Kofi walks his fire across the floor.

They come out into a wider gallery — and the passage ahead necks down to a gap four feet wide.

Kofi stops at it. Turns around. Looks back the way they came.

He doesn't set up. He's already set up.

KOFI
Go on, then.

RYAN
No.

KOFI
Corridor's four feet wide, Ryan. I've got a belt and a good angle.

He kneels into the gun.

KOFI (CONT'D)
That's the best day I've had in ten years.

RYAN
Kofi—

KOFI
You asked Marcus to tell you when you were pushing one more klick.

He racks the belt.

KOFI (CONT'D)
You didn't ask. So I'm telling you. Go.

Maya pulls Ryan through the gap.

Behind them the machine gun starts, and it does not stop, and it goes on for a very long time.

And then it stops.

INT. LOWER PASSAGE - RIVER LEVEL - CONTINUOUS

*[DEPTH 7 — knee-deep, black, and rising. Roof coming down to meet the water.]*

Three of them at the mouth of the passage they came in by.

It's gone. The river has come up to the roof. Black water, no gap, no light.

Marcus puts his hand flat on the surface. It's moving fast.

MARCUS
It's still rising.

Maya is looking at Ryan, and Ryan is looking at the water, and there is a long silence.

RYAN
We're not hunting it any more.

He turns around and looks back into the mountain.

RYAN (CONT'D)
It's hunting us.

INT. SLUICE PASSAGE - LATER

*[DEPTH 8 — waist-deep and moving fast. Rotted timber. The loudest place in the film.]*

A century-old timber gate, half rotted, holding back a wall of water in a stone channel. Beyond it, a passage that climbs.

A hand winch beside the gate, chain and pawl, crusted with rust.

Marcus reads it in three seconds.

MARCUS
It won't lock. Somebody works the winch, the other two get through.

RYAN
Then somebody's on this side.

MARCUS
Yes.

Ryan puts his hand on the winch.

And Maya sets her pack down on the stone.

Ryan sees her do it, and he understands, and it takes the strength out of his legs.

RYAN
No.

MAYA
Marcus, get in the channel.

RYAN
Maya. No.

MAYA
It's the same thing you'd do if you were faster than me.

RYAN
I'm not letting—

MAYA
You're not letting.

She almost laughs. She takes his face in both hands.

MAYA (CONT'D)
You said it back to me. On the dock.

RYAN
I said I'd bring you home. I said it.

MAYA
You said it and you meant it. That was the whole thing I wanted. I already got it.

She lets go.

MAYA (CONT'D)
So go home and be somebody who means things.

She takes the winch handle out of his hands.

MAYA (CONT'D)
Marcus. Move him.

Marcus moves him.

INT. CLIMBING PASSAGE - CONTINUOUS

*[DEPTH 9 — climbing out of the water. Wet rock, running walls, the sound of the flood dropping away below.]*

Ryan and Marcus haul up out of the channel, soaked, into the rising passage.

Behind and below, the gate holds. Maya on the winch, both arms, feet braced.

Her radio is in her collar. Same dead channel as the settlement.

MAYA (V.O.)
(over radio, calm)
I've got maybe four minutes on this.

RYAN
(into radio)
Maya, listen to me—

MAYA (V.O.)
Ryan. She's here.

Ryan stops climbing.

MAYA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
She's in the channel with me. She's about — God. She's beautiful.

Marcus takes hold of Ryan's webbing.

MAYA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Ryan. She went past me.

Silence.

MAYA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
She went right past me. I'm standing in the open and she didn't even slow down.

RYAN
Maya—

MAYA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
She's not following us. She's following what we took.

Static rises.

MAYA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Drop them. Ryan — drop them.

RYAN
Maya!

MAYA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Drop th—

The gate goes.

The sound of an entire river arriving at once, and then nothing on the channel at all.

Ryan hangs there in the dark on the rock.

Marcus watches him. Waits.

MARCUS
Ryan.

Ryan doesn't move.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
Your pack.

Ryan looks down at it. Forty pounds. A lifetime. Six people.

His hands close on the straps.

And he does not take it off.

Marcus sees that. Says nothing. Turns and climbs.

INT. THE DEEP CHAMBER - LATER

*[DEPTH 10 — THE ONLY ROOM THAT LIGHTS ITSELF. Diamond-scatter from every wall. Dry, vast, and beautiful. Kill the torches.]*

They come out of a crack in the wall into a space that stops both of them dead.

It is vast. And the walls are solid light — diamonds in the rock from floor to roof, in seams and sheets and constellations, a whole sky of them, all the way up into the dark.

And at the centre of the floor, in a shallow hollow, arranged — deliberately, carefully, in a spiral —

THE CLUTCH. Hundreds of them.

Marcus lowers his rifle.

MARCUS
Oh, son.

Ryan can't speak.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
Look at how she put them down. Look how careful.

And the far wall moves.

It is so slow that for three full seconds it reads as the room settling. Then a length of it lifts, and lifts, and keeps lifting, and the light from their torches runs down two thousand years of wet scale, over the old healed scars, over the pale slash across the left side of the head.

Fifty feet of her. Thicker through the middle than a man's chest.

The chamber is enormous. She is not bigger than it. She is the thing it was built around.

She raises her head above them.

And she goes past Ryan.

Straight past him. To the hollow. To the eggs. She lowers her head to them and moves along the spiral, checking, counting.

Marcus, not moving his lips:

MARCUS (CONT'D)
Maya was right.

RYAN
(whisper)
She's counting them.

MARCUS
And she's going to come up short. Forty pounds short.

Ryan's hand goes to his pack strap.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
Too late for that now, son.

Because the great head has stopped.

And it turns. And it comes around. And it finds Ryan, and the pack on his back, and it does not blink.

Marcus steps out from the wall into open ground.

RYAN
Marcus. Marcus, don't—

MARCUS
You've got charges and you've got a roof.

He raises the rifle at an animal half as long again as the boat that brought them here.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
Go one more klick.

And he fires.

The chamber ANSWERS — muzzle flash, sound folding back off stone, the enormous body coming around, and Marcus standing in the middle of it not moving an inch.

The head comes down.

Marcus is gone.

INT. TUNNELS - CONTINUOUS

*[DEPTH 10 — leaving the chamber. Torch on the ground, light thrown sideways. Shallow water underfoot.]*

RYAN RUNS.

And this is not a man escaping. This is a man working.

He goes where she cannot follow at speed — the narrow seams, the low crawls, the switchbacks where fifty feet of body has to double back on itself to follow.

He pulls Mateo's charges out of the pack as he moves, setting them by feel, jamming them up into the fracture lines in the roof, paying out wire.

Behind him, the sound of something vast having to slow down. Having to choose a way around.

He drops into shallow water in a low tunnel, and works, and his hands are steady.

Then he keys the radio in his collar. The dead channel. Nobody on it.

RYAN
(quietly)
Maya.

Static.

RYAN (CONT'D)
I'm putting them down.

He unslings the pack.

INT. UNDERGROUND TUNNEL - CONTINUOUS

*[DEPTH 11 — matches the cold open exactly. Same tunnel, same handprints, same failing torch.]*

And we are here again.

The handprints on the wall. The flashlight. The blood down the side of his face. The rifle still slung across his back, still not in his hands.

He has understood, by now, that it was never going to be the answer.

Far behind him, down the tunnel — the grey smudge of daylight.

He looks at it.

And turns his back on it, because the daylight is the wrong way. The charges are the other way.

He takes the last egg-sized diamond out of the pack. Sets it down on the wet stone.

Carefully. Almost gently.

And backs away from it.

HOLD on it in the water. Inside, something turns over.

The HISS. The weight of her shifting in the dark.

Ryan does not run. He watches the stone.

Two eyes rise out of the black — and go to it. Not to him.

She comes forward. All of her — well over a ton moving through shallow water without hurrying, going to the one egg on the floor.

Ryan stands flat against the wall among the handprints, and fifty feet of her passes eighteen inches from his face, and it takes a very long time.

RYAN
(whisper)
We were never supposed to find them.

He turns and runs — deeper. Away from the daylight.

He hits the crawl. He gets through it. He comes up in the fracture seam with the wire in his hand.

Behind him the whole tunnel is moving toward the sound of him.

He puts his back against the rock, and closes his eyes, and pulls.

EXT. ISLAND - SINKHOLE - CONTINUOUS

The jungle floor JUMPS.

Birds that were not there a second ago come off the canopy in a sheet.

The sinkhole exhales a column of dust two hundred feet into the morning air.

And then the island is silent again, the way it was when they came.

EXT. RIVER - MORNING

The boat, where they left it, sitting in flat brown water.

The treeline moves.

Ryan comes out of it.

Mud to the chest, blood dried black, one boot gone. He walks to the boat and he does not look back at the island, not once.

He gets in. Sits down. Doesn't start the engine.

He opens his pack.

Inside: THREE ENORMOUS DIAMONDS.

The sun comes over the trees and hits them and the whole boat fills with light.

Ryan looks at them.

He looks at them long enough for it to be six people and three stones.

His hands begin to shake.

He closes the bag.

INT. SUBURBAN DOORWAY - DAY

A screen door. A quiet street somewhere hot.

A WOMAN, 30s, opens it. Behind her, a kid's bicycle on its side in the hallway.

She looks at Ryan. She looks at him for about two seconds, which is all it takes.

WOMAN
Where is he?

Ryan opens his mouth.

WOMAN (CONT'D)
Where is my husband?

He has nothing. Not one word.

He puts a thick envelope down on the step, between them, and it is the most useless object in the world.

She doesn't look at it.

WOMAN (CONT'D)
He said you'd bring him back.

Ryan turns and walks down the path.

INT. RYAN'S ROOM - DAY

The same bare rented room. Curtains shut against the afternoon. A jeweller's lamp clamped to the table, and black velvet laid over the wood.

One diamond on it. The buyer came to him.

The BUYER, 60s, has been looking at it through a loupe without moving for a full minute.

He puts the loupe down.

BUYER
Where did you get this.

Ryan says nothing.

BUYER (CONT'D)
Let me ask it a different way. Is there more.

RYAN
No.

The buyer looks at him. Then back at the stone.

BUYER
I've been doing this for forty years.

He turns it under the lamp.

BUYER (CONT'D)
There is no mine on earth that produces something like this.

RYAN
I know.

INT. RYAN'S ROOM - NIGHT

The same room, hours later. The velvet gone. The lamp off.

On the table, on a folded cloth: the last TWO DIAMONDS.

Ryan asleep on the couch, still in his boots.

His eyes open.

He doesn't move. He's listening.

Quiet. Traffic somewhere. A fridge.

And then, very small, very close:

A HISS.

Ryan sits up.

He looks at the table.

The two diamonds are exactly where he left them.

He crosses the room. Stands over them.

And in the second stone, running through the middle of it, there is a THIN DARK LINE that was not there before.

He leans closer.

CLOSE ON the stone. The line is not a flaw.

THE LINE MOVES.

Ryan steps back.

And somewhere out past the window — a long, long way away, out over the dark — something enormous ANSWERS.

CUT TO BLACK.

THE END.
